codex
Built the Track B generator, but it is not yet shippable because the required oracle calls are blocked by exhausted OpenRouter quota.

The native family asks for a \(K_4[t]\) blowup under Theorem 2.4’s signless-Laplacian regime. Certificates are transported through an exact reversible GF(2) relabelling, never discovered by search. [Paper PDF](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.02263)

- [Generator](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2507.02263/gen_2507_02263.py)
- [Self-test report](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2507.02263/selftest_report.json)
- [README](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2507.02263/README.md)
- [Bare transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2507.02263/llm_loop_transcript.jsonl)
- [Hinted transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2507.02263/g9_hinted_transcript.jsonl)
- [Placebo transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2507.02263/g9_placebo_transcript.jsonl)

Local results:

- G1–G8 pass.
- Random guessing: 0/200,000.
- Five attacks: 0/8 each.
- Reference algorithm: 4,632 exact operations.
- Compact route: 240 operations.
- Answer: 440 characters, 48 elements.
- G9 remains `pending_api_error`.

All three hardening runs were attempted with the mandated harness; every redraw returned HTTP 403 `Key limit exceeded (total limit)`. These errors are preserved as errors rather than falsely counted as oracle failures. No rejection or hardness verdict is claimed.
