    18	
    19	## What the problem is and why it is trustworthy
    20	
    21	The source is Zheng, Li, and Fan, [*Some Turán-type results for the signless
    22	Laplacian spectral radius*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.02263). Section 1.3 defines
    23	the paper’s central object: $Q(G)=D(G)+A(G)$ and its largest eigenvalue $q(G)$.
    24	Section 1.1 defines complete multipartite Turán graphs and clique blowups; these
    25	objects drive the results in Section 2. An instance gives a randomly relabelled
    26	complete multipartite graph by exact hexadecimal adjacency rows. The solver must
    27	return $q(G)$ as its monic integer minimal polynomial and an open isolating
    28	interval with integer endpoints.
    29	
    30	If the distinct part sizes are $s_1,\ldots,s_k$, $N=\sum_i s_i$, and the
    31	Perron vector has value $x_i$ on part $i$, then its eigen-equations give
    32	
    33	\[
    34	  (q-N+2s_i)x_i=S,\qquad
    35	  \sum_i \frac{s_i}{q-N+2s_i}=1.
    36	\]
    37	
    38	Clearing denominators gives, by composition, the certificate polynomial
    39	
    40	\[
    41	 P(X)=\prod_i(X-N+2s_i)-\sum_i s_i\prod_{j\ne i}(X-N+2s_j).
    42	\]
    43	
    44	The generator samples the part sizes first and keeps only vectors for which a
    45	small-prime Rabin test proves $P$ irreducible modulo that prime. Gauss’s lemma
    46	then proves that $P$ is the minimal polynomial over ℚ. Its Perron root is the
    47	unique root above the largest pole $N-2\min s_i$, and $q(G)<2(N-1)$. Thus the
    48	answer is known without solving the emitted matrix. `verify` reconstructs the
    49	parts from adjacency rows, recomputes $P$, reruns the finite-field
    50	irreducibility proof, and uses exact Sturm counting both inside and above the
    51	submitted interval to certify that it isolates the largest real root. It never
    52	reads the planted answer and accepts every valid isolating interval in the
    53	declared language.
    54	
    55	## Why this is Track B
    56	
    57	This cannot honestly be Track A: exact eigenvalue algorithms exist. The measured
    58	reference route builds the full integer matrix, computes its characteristic
    59	polynomial by exact Faddeev–LeVerrier, removes rational eigenvalue factors, and
    60	isolates the largest remaining root by Sturm’s theorem. It is polynomial time,
    61	$O(N^4)$ exact scalar operations, and solved 8/8 base-preset instances. At
    62	$N=56,k=7$, it averaged 16,141,202 counted operations and 0.859335 seconds
    63	(maximum 16,765,266 and 0.937650 seconds) on the final audit run.
    64	
    65	The compact route notices that identical adjacency rows are the multipartite
    66	cells, counts their seven sizes, and accumulates the denominator and weighted
    67	numerator of the displayed rank-one Perron identity together. Including all 56
    68	row-count increments, it uses 226 exact arithmetic operations. That is within
    69	the no-tool cap once the decomposition is seen; the 16-million-operation matrix
    70	route is not executable by hand in the evaluation context.
    71	
    72	The paper’s Theorem 2.4 does not itself make a finite benchmark: it guarantees a
    73	$K_{k+1}[t]$ only for sufficiently large $n$, with hidden constants, and
    74	Example 4.1 proves the logarithmic $t$-scale sharp. The theorem also fails for
    75	$k=1$, as the discussion after Theorem 2.4 explains. Rather than claim a modest
    76	planted blowup is theorem-backed, this family stays with the native spectral
    77	quantity and uses an exact composition certificate.
    78	
    79	## Worked demo
    80	
